WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.400
In many two-player games, you can work out who should

00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.070
win before the game starts.

00:00:05.860 --> 00:00:08.580
Label each position as winning or losing for the player

00:00:08.580 --> 00:00:11.100
about to move, beginning with the smallest positions

00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:12.230
and working up.

00:00:13.080 --> 00:00:15.360
Playing out every possible game is hopeless.

00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:18.940
Labelling positions needs only a little work for each

00:00:18.940 --> 00:00:21.300
one, and it tells you the best move as well

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:21.910
as the winner.

00:00:22.840 --> 00:00:26.020
Two players take turns removing one or two counters from

00:00:26.020 --> 00:00:26.950
a pile of ten.

00:00:27.460 --> 00:00:29.290
Whoever takes the last counter wins.

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:31.690
Would you rather go first or second?

00:00:32.300 --> 00:00:34.190
Pause and think about very small piles.

00:00:38.170 --> 00:00:41.100
With one or two counters, you take them all and win.

00:00:41.690 --> 00:00:42.560
Those are winning positions.

00:00:43.570 --> 00:00:46.290
With three, whatever you take, you leave one or two,

00:00:46.290 --> 00:00:47.640
and your opponent wins.

00:00:48.430 --> 00:00:49.680
Three is a losing position.

00:00:50.650 --> 00:00:54.050
The rule is: a position is winning if some move leads to

00:00:54.050 --> 00:00:57.230
a losing position, and losing if every move leads to

00:00:57.230 --> 00:00:58.200
a winning position.

00:00:58.810 --> 00:01:00.780
From four or five you can move to three,

00:01:00.950 --> 00:01:01.720
so they are winning.

00:01:02.350 --> 00:01:02.960
Six is losing.

00:01:03.610 --> 00:01:06.560
The losing positions are the multiples of three: three,

00:01:06.770 --> 00:01:07.780
six and nine.

00:01:08.730 --> 00:01:12.190
Ten is not a multiple of three, so go first and take

00:01:12.190 --> 00:01:13.480
one, leaving nine.

00:01:14.150 --> 00:01:17.290
After that, whatever your opponent takes, you take the

00:01:17.290 --> 00:01:18.860
amount that makes three altogether.

00:01:19.430 --> 00:01:21.870
You always leave a multiple of three, and you take

00:01:21.870 --> 00:01:22.820
the last counter.

00:01:23.930 --> 00:01:26.980
Change the rule to taking one, two or three.

00:01:27.570 --> 00:01:30.090
One, two and three are winning, since you can

00:01:30.090 --> 00:01:30.780
take them all.

00:01:31.290 --> 00:01:31.900
Four is losing.

00:01:32.950 --> 00:01:35.770
From a multiple of four, any move lets the next player

00:01:35.770 --> 00:01:37.780
finish a total removal of four.

00:01:38.190 --> 00:01:40.530
From any other size, take enough to reach a

00:01:40.530 --> 00:01:41.520
multiple of four.

00:01:42.010 --> 00:01:44.380
The losing positions are the multiples of four.

00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:48.050
A position is winning if you can move to a losing one.

00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:50.950
It is losing if every move goes to a winning one.

00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:52.650
Start labelling from the end.

00:01:53.320 --> 00:01:56.040
Notebook challenge: with the take one or two rule and a

00:01:56.040 --> 00:01:58.850
pile of fourteen, what should the first player take?
